(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-2-acute-vision-loss-vignettes"]={"title":"Acute Vision Loss Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 2","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 79-year-old man has sudden vision loss in one eye. Which referral threshold does the lecture apply?","choices":["Emergent referral for anyone over 50","Routine referral within a month","Referral only with a headache","Referral only if markers are raised","Referral only if both eyes are involved"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, any patient over 50 with sudden visual loss. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with a retinal detachment suddenly loses vision in the whole eye rather than just a segment. Which development explains this?","choices":["The optic nerve has demyelinated","A second tear has formed anteriorly","The vitreous has cleared","The macula has become involved","The intraocular pressure has risen"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct; macular involvement causes sudden loss in the affected eye. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with obesity and poorly controlled hypertension notices his vision in one eye has blurred progressively over about a week, without pain. The fundus is filled with haemorrhage. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Central retinal artery occlusion","Amaurosis fugax","Angle-closure glaucoma","Retinal detachment","Central retinal vein occlusion"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct; loss may be gradual over days to weeks in some patients. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman has papilledema. Which imaging does the lecture obtain, and why?","choices":["Orbital ultrasound, to type a detachment","MRI of the orbits, to find demyelination","Carotid Doppler, to find plaque","No imaging is required","MRI or CT of the head, to exclude a mass"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with atrial fibrillation has profound painless loss of vision in the right eye that came on over a few seconds. He can only count fingers. The retina is pale and swollen with a cherry-red spot at the fovea. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Central retinal vein occlusion","Amaurosis fugax","Retinal detachment","Optic neuritis","Central retinal artery occlusion"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Sudden profound loss with the cherry-red fovea. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with a central retinal artery occlusion asks about his general health risk. What does the lecture emphasise?","choices":["His stroke risk is markedly increased","His stroke risk is unchanged","His risk is confined to the other eye","His risk falls once vision is lost","His risk depends on intraocular pressure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct; plaque reaching the retinal artery implies more in the carotids. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man with sleep apnea wakes to find the upper half of vision in one eye missing. He has no pain, no headache and no scalp tenderness. Inflammatory markers are normal. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Branch retinal vein occlusion","Amaurosis fugax","Non-arteritic anterior ischemic optic neuropathy","Arteritic anterior ischemic optic neuropathy","Optic neuritis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct; painless altitudinal loss with normal markers. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman has had a second episode of optic neuritis in two years. Which point should you raise with her?","choices":["Recurrence means the first episode was misdiagnosed","Recurrence means she will go blind","Recurrence indicates glaucoma","Recurrence requires no further workup","Recurrence raises her risk of multiple sclerosis"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct, which is why the underlying cause is pursued. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man is starting a long corticosteroid course for arteritic optic neuropathy. Which additional drug does the lecture add?","choices":["Pilocarpine","Famotidine","Acetazolamide","Latanoprost","Warfarin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, for gastrointestinal ulcer prophylaxis. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman keeps bumping into door frames on her left. She has noticed nothing wrong with her reading vision and has no pain. Both intraocular pressures are 27 mmHg. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Optic neuritis","Retinal detachment","Papilledema","Chronic open-angle glaucoma","Acute angle-closure glaucoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct; peripheral field goes first while central vision is preserved. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man has bilateral disc swelling. Which test does the lecture use to confirm that intracranial pressure is raised?","choices":["Carotid Doppler","Lumbar puncture opening pressure","Tonometry","Fluorescein angiography","Erythrocyte sedimentation rate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, after imaging has excluded a mass lesion. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 63-year-old myopic man reports a shower of floaters and flashing lights three days ago. Since yesterday a dark shadow has spread across the outer half of his vision. There is no pain. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Angle-closure glaucoma","Retinal detachment","Amaurosis fugax","Central retinal vein occlusion","Optic neuritis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Flashes and floaters from the tear, then an advancing field defect. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman had numbness in one leg last year that resolved. She now has aching behind one eye that worsens when she looks around, with vision fading over two days. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Angle-closure glaucoma","Central retinal vein occlusion","Papilledema","Optic neuritis","Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct; the prior neurological episode points to a demyelinating cause. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has a suspected retinal detachment but the view on fundoscopy is poor. Which study does the lecture prefer?","choices":["Gonioscopy","Lumbar puncture","Ocular ultrasound","Fluorescein angiography","MRI of the orbits"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, more sensitive than fundoscopy and it types the detachment. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man has amaurosis fugax and you want to reduce his stroke risk. Which therapy does the lecture name?","choices":["Oral acetazolamide","High-dose vitamin A","Pilocarpine drops","Aspirin and clopidogrel","Topical timolol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, given to reduce cerebrovascular accident risk. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man with optic nerve changes has an intraocular pressure of 18 mmHg. How does the lecture describe pressure in the chronic open-angle form?","choices":["It is always below normal","It cannot be measured in this form","It may be normal or elevated","It is always above 40 mmHg","It is always normal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct; nerve damage occurs with or without raised pressure. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 65-year-old man with a painful eye has tonometry performed. Which pressure range does the lecture give for acute angle closure?","choices":["Pressure is characteristically normal","40 to 80 mmHg","10 to 21 mmHg","22 to 30 mmHg","90 to 120 mmHg"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with hypertension and diabetes has sudden painless loss of vision in one eye. Fundoscopy shows a swollen disc, dilated veins, cotton wool spots and haemorrhages across all four quadrants. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Papilledema","Optic neuritis","Central retinal vein occlusion","Central retinal artery occlusion","Retinal detachment"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The blood and thunder fundus with disc swelling. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman presents 40 minutes after sudden painless monocular vision loss. Why is she treated immediately?","choices":["Retinal damage is irreversible after 24 hours","Damage is reversible at any point","Damage occurs only if both eyes are affected","Retinal damage is irreversible after 90 minutes","Retinal damage is irreversible after 8 hours"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct; this is the window the lecture gives. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man has transient monocular vision loss and you suspect a carotid source. Which study does the lecture pair with that suspicion?","choices":["Carotid Doppler ultrasound","Echocardiogram","Tonometry","Lumbar puncture","Temporal artery biopsy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Echocardiography is the study when a cardiac source is suspected. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman has bilateral optic disc swelling. Which diagnosis does bilateral involvement most suggest?","choices":["Retinal detachment","Optic neuritis","Amaurosis fugax","Papilledema","Central retinal artery occlusion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct; the vascular occlusions and detachment are monocular. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman is newly diagnosed with chronic open-angle glaucoma. Which does the lecture list as first-line?","choices":["Anterior chamber paracentesis","Latanoprost","Laser peripheral iridotomy","Intravenous mannitol","Oral corticosteroids"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with tafluprost and timolol drops. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with a hypercoagulable disorder develops a central retinal vein occlusion. What has occurred?","choices":["An embolus has lodged in the artery","The iris has closed the drainage angle","The retina has separated from its bed","Intracranial pressure has risen","A thrombus has occluded the vein"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct, preventing the retina from draining. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman has sudden severe right eye pain, a headache, nausea and vomiting. She sees coloured halos around lights and her cornea looks hazy. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Central retinal artery occlusion","Amaurosis fugax","Acute angle-closure glaucoma","Chronic open-angle glaucoma","Optic neuritis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pain, halos and systemic upset with a hazy cornea. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man has acute angle-closure glaucoma. What is happening inside his eye?","choices":["The iris blocks the drainage circuit","The trabecular meshwork ages abnormally","The retina separates from its bed","Intracranial pressure compresses the disc","A thrombus occludes the central vein"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and intraocular pressure rises dramatically. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with amaurosis fugax asks what happens next. What does the lecture give as the outlook?","choices":["The second eye is always affected","About 85% recover fully","Nearly all progress to permanent blindness","About half lose vision permanently","Recovery depends on surgery"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the remainder progress to a central retinal artery occlusion. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man has a central retinal artery occlusion. His affected pupil is sluggish to direct light but constricts briskly when the other eye is illuminated. What did the lecturer say about this finding?","choices":["It is a major clinical clue","It excludes an arterial cause","It indicates raised intracranial pressure","It indicates a detached retina","It is a normal finding"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct; she singled out this sentence in the lecture. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman with optic neuritis has an MRI showing three characteristic demyelinating lesions. What does the lecture say happens next?","choices":["Temporal artery biopsy","Lumbar puncture to lower pressure","Referral to neurology or neuro-ophthalmology","Reassurance and discharge","Immediate laser iridotomy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, once at least two such lesions are present. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 65-year-old woman with sickle cell disease describes brief episodes in which the lower half of the vision in one eye fogs over and then clears within a minute. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Branch retinal artery occlusion","Retinal detachment","Optic neuritis","Papilledema","Amaurosis fugax"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct; sickle cell is a listed risk factor and the episodes are transient. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with untreated chronic open-angle glaucoma says he feels as though he is looking down a tube. Which finding does this describe?","choices":["Loss of peripheral vision first","Loss of central vision first","Loss of colour vision","A curtain descending from above","Transient blackout"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct; patients call it tunnel vision, and central sight is preserved longest. — 12. Acute Vision Loss current - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 16"}]};
